Back to Articles Welcome Inkling 2 Mini, Cutting-edge Performance for your Local Setup Published November 20, 2026 Update on GitHub Upvote 18 +12 merve merve Follow Pedro Cuenca pcuenq Follow Aritra Roy Gosthipaty ariG23498 Follow Guarin Flück guarin Follow TLDR; Inkling 2 Mini is a new looped transformer model. It’s a multimodal Mixture-of-Experts with ~26B physical total and 3B active parameters per token. It uses a neat trick to increase capacity: some layers are visited in a loop that repeats three times, which makes it work like it had more parameters. It supports a 1M-token context size, accepts images and text inputs. Thinking Machines Lab ships the model in a mixed-precision format, with NVFP4 and MXFP8 expert weights. A DSpark drafter is also included for additional speed-up at a small memory cost. Inkling 2 Mini is much, much smaller than Inkling 2 Small, and is designed for local AI setups. We think this is a refreshingly new great local model. In our vibe tests, it's at least comparable with Qwen 3.8 27B. More validation is needed, but we are super excited about this release! We ship transformers, Llama App (llama.cpp), and MLX support. SGLang also provides day-zero inference support for production deployments on datacenter hardware. This post goes through what makes Inkling Mini different, some of our vibe tests, and how you can use it with your setup. What is new with Inkling 2 Mini? Inkling 2 Mini is a mixture-of-experts model with 24 decoder layers, but the 12 middle layers run three times in a loop. Therefore, the model acts as if it had 48 (6+ 3*12 + 6) logical layers with just 24 physical ones. Similar to Inkling 2 Small, Inkling 2 Mini executes 48 layer visits per token. The hidden size is 2,304 instead of 4,608. At each layer visit, both variants route the token to 8 experts, but Mini selects them from among 128 experts per layer, while Small selects from 384. The decoder runs layers 0–5 once, layers 6–17 three times, and layers 18–23 once. All three passes through layers 6–17 reuse the same weights. Architectures such as Nanbeige have explored looping before. But Inkling 2 Mini explores an interesting approach. Every pass through the looped core (layers 6–17) starts from the same inputs which is the output of the first six layers. Information moves between passes through cross-pass messages . Each looped layer reads a message from the previous pass's outputs with its own learned query (one message for its query, key, value and MLP inputs). A small gating controller then looks at both the layer's current input and the message. It decides how much of the current input to forget and how much of the message to add. Each pass therefore refines what the previous one computed. Each six-layer block has five sliding-window layers and one full-attention layer. The local window holds up to 1,024 tokens. Attention uses 24 query heads and eight KV heads. It uses learned relative position logits, a learned sink, and an output gate. Full-attention layers use dynamic key and value convolutions. The router selects eight of 128 SwiGLU experts for each token. It normalizes the selected scores and scales the weights by 8. During passes 2 and 3, each core layer attends over two residual blocks from the previous pass. Four rank-64 gates mix separate messages into the query, key, value, and MoE inputs. Text decoder width is 2,304, and the intermediate width of each expert is 1,152. Reusing core weights reduces the number of distinct layers the checkpoint stores. The model still executes all 48 visits, and each visit keeps its own KV and short-convolution state. In addition to the flagship Inkling 2 Mini model, a research artifact has also been released. It shares the same architecture, but this variant was trained to accept a variable number of passes through the loop 🤯. Setting 1 or 2 passes instead of 3 will make inference run a lot faster, but generations may be of slightly lower quality. We tested this variant, and it works, but we haven’t evaluated it in depth. Benchmark Results Inkling 2 Mini is state-of-the-art in its class in the Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index v5 (AAv5), as well as across many benchmarks in various categories. Compared with Gemma 4 31B, Muse Glimmer, Qwen3.8-27B, and Nemotron 3.5 Super VL, Inkling 2 Mini leads in 32 benchmarks out of 48. These cover coding and autoresearch, agentic search and tool use for knowledge work, vision interpretation and creation, reasoning and knowledge, and safety and alignment. See the Inkling 2 Mini model card for the full list of benchmarks and scores. Inkling 2 Mini is a great local coding companion. It leads in FrontierCode v2.0, ties with Qwen3.8-27B on Terminal-Bench 4.0, and trails Qwen on AutomationBench-AA v1.0.6. You can check out a few of our highlights below for coding and knowledge work. Note that AA Briefcase is in ELO rating, higher is better. Inference Support In this section, you can find how to use Inkling 2 Mini in transformers, llama.cpp, MLX, and SGLang. Transformers We provide support for both Inkling 2 Mini (fixed loop) and the TML-d24-varloop-research (variable loop) research variant. Fixed loop with Inkling 2 Mini You can use the AutoModelForMultimodalLM class. import torch from transformers import AutoModelForMultimodalLM, AutoProcessor model_id = "thinkingmachines/Inkling-2-Mini" processor = AutoProcessor.from_pretrained(model_id)
model = AutoModelForMultimodalLM.from_pretrained( model_id,
) messages = [{ "role" : "user" , "content" : [{ "type" : "text" , "text" : "Why is the sky blue?" }]}] inputs = processor.apply_chat_template( messages, tokenize= True , add_generation_prompt= True , return_dict= True , return_tensors= "pt" ,
).to(model.device)
input_len = inputs[ "input_ids" ].shape[- 1 ] outputs = model.generate(**inputs, max_new_tokens= 1024 ) print (processor.decode(outputs[ 0 ][input_len:], skip_special_tokens= False )) Variable loop To set the number of loops in the research checkpoint, you can modify the loop_num_passes in text_config as follows. The example below uses two loops. import torch from transformers import AutoModelForMultimodalLM, AutoProcessor model_id = "thinkingmachines/TML-d24-varloop-research" processor = AutoProcessor.from_pretrained(model_id)
model = AutoModelForMultimodalLM.from_pretrained( model_id, text_config={ "loop_num_passes" : 2 },
) messages = [{ "role" : "user" , "content" : [{ "type" : "text" , "text" : "Why is the sky blue?" }]}] inputs = processor.apply_chat_template( messages, reasoning_effort= "medium" , tokenize= True , add_generation_prompt= True , return_dict= True , return_tensors= "pt" , ).to(model.device)
input_len = inputs[ "input_ids" ].shape[- 1 ] outputs = model.generate(**inputs, max_new_tokens= 1024 ) print (processor.decode(outputs[ 0 ][input_len:], skip_special_tokens= False )) Images and text For an image prompt, replace messages in either example with the list below and infer as usual. messages = [ { "role" : "user" , "content" : [ { "type" : "image" , "image" : "/path/to/image.jpg" }, { "type" : "text" , "text" : "What is in this image?" }, ], }
] Reasoning Effort Pass reasoning_effort="medium" to processor.apply_chat_template(...) to set the effort for a request. The chat template adds the numeric value to the prompt as a system message. The default is high (0.9). inputs = processor.apply_chat_template( messages, reasoning_effort= "medium" , tokenize= True , add_generation_prompt= True , return_dict= True , return_tensors= "pt" , ).to(model.device) Available levels are none (0.0), minimal (0.1), low (0.2), medium (0.7), high (0.9; default), xhigh (0.99), and max (0.99). You can also pass a number from 0.0 to 0.99, such as reasoning_effort=0.35 . Tool calling Let’s get our model to call some tools. In this example we ask our model about the current temperature at a given location. With the prompt, we also provide the tool ( get_current_temperature ) to do it.

Summary Qwen3 TTS on Together AI: Qwen's open text-to-speech model, on Together Serverless Inference and dedicated endpoints at \$20 per 1M characters. One voice, many deliveries: nine preset voices that change tone and emotion from a plain-language instruction such as "excited and upbeat." 10 languages per voice: every preset speaks Chinese, English, Japanese, Korean, German, French, Russian, Portuguese, Spanish and Italian. Built for real-time voice: first audio in as little as 97ms, with HTTP streaming and a WebSocket API for text that arrives in pieces. Starting today, developers can run Qwen3 TTS on Together AI, with first audio in as little as 97ms. Qwen3 TTS is an open text-to-speech model that separates who is speaking from how they speak. You choose one of nine preset voices, and that voice stays the same while you change its delivery with a plain-language instruction, such as "excited and upbeat," or move it between 10 languages. Together AI's inference team tuned the serving stack for Qwen3 TTS so that speech starts streaming quickly enough for real-time conversation. The model is available now on Together Serverless Inference and on dedicated endpoints. Tell it how to say the line The same sentence can land as good news or bad news depending on how it's said, and with Qwen3 TTS you decide which by describing the delivery in a few words. Here the voice aiden reads one line three times. The voice and the words don't change between clips, only the instruction. aiden · excited and upbeat Play Pause 0:00 0:02 "Well, that changes everything." aiden · angry and impatient Play Pause 0:00 0:02 "Well, that changes everything." aiden · sad and disappointed Play Pause 0:00 0:02 "Well, that changes everything." The instruction controls the delivery, and the voice stays the one you chose. You can keep that same voice when you change languages too. Keep one voice across 10 languages Every preset voice speaks all 10 supported languages: Chinese, English, Japanese, Korean, German, French, Russian, Portuguese, Spanish and Italian. That lets a product keep the same voice when it moves into a new market. Here aiden , an English voice, reads the same order update in four languages. aiden · English (en) Play Pause 0:00 0:03 "Your order has shipped and should arrive on Thursday." aiden · Spanish (es) Play Pause 0:00 0:03 "Tu pedido ha sido enviado y debería llegar el jueves." aiden · German (de) Play Pause 0:00 0:04 "Ihre Bestellung wurde versandt und sollte am Donnerstag ankommen." aiden · Japanese (ja) Play Pause 0:00 0:05 "ご註文の商品は発送済みで、木曜日に到著する予定です。" Supply the text in the language you select; the four clips above use separately translated text. Each voice also has a native language, and Qwen recommends using a voice in its native language for the best quality, so listen to the languages your application needs before you settle on a preset. Choose a voice in VoiceFinder The quickest way to choose a preset is to hear each one say your own lines. VoiceFinder lists the Qwen3 TTS voices, plays a sample of each and reads the text you enter in any of them. Open voicefinder.together.ai , select Qwen/Qwen3-TTS-12Hz-1.7B in the model menu and select Find voices . Select Preview to play a sample of a voice. Select Try custom text , enter up to 500 characters and select Generate to hear that voice read your text. Repeat with another voice to compare them on the same text. Select Show code to get the request for that voice in Python, TypeScript or cURL. The nine preset voices, with each one's native language and Qwen's description: Voice Native language Description aiden English Sunny American male voice with a clear midrange ryan English Dynamic male voice with strong rhythmic drive vivian Chinese Bright, slightly edgy young female voice serena Chinese Warm, gentle young female voice uncle_fu Chinese Seasoned male voice with a low, mellow timbre dylan Chinese, Beijing dialect Youthful Beijing male voice with a clear, natural timbre eric Chinese, Sichuan dialect Lively Chengdu male voice with a slightly husky brightness ono_anna Japanese Playful Japanese female voice with a light, nimble timbre sohee Korean Warm Korean female voice with rich emotion Generate speech You need the Together Python SDK ( pip install together ) and a TOGETHER_API_KEY . This request sets the voice, the language and the instruction together, and produces the excited reading of the line above. from together import Together client = Together() response = client.audio.speech.create( model="Qwen/Qwen3-TTS-12Hz-1.7B", input="Well, that changes everything.", voice="aiden", # who speaks language="en", # the language of the text extra_body={"instructions": "excited and upbeat"}, # how to say it response_format="mp3",
) response.write_to_file("excited.mp3") The Python SDK passes instructions through extra_body . If an instruction doesn't seem to change the delivery, check that the field is spelled instructions , since a misspelled field is skipped without an error. Parameters for this model: Parameter What to send voice One of the nine voice names, lowercase instructions A short description of the delivery, such as excited and upbeat . Optional language ISO 639-1 code for the input text, such as en , zh , ja or ko response_format wav , mp3 , raw or mulaw . opus , aac and flac aren't supported sample_rate 24000 (default) or 8000 Word timestamps ( alignment=word ) aren't supported for Qwen3 TTS. Stream audio for a voice agent In a voice agent, speech has to start before the whole reply has been synthesized. On Together AI, Qwen3 TTS returns first audio in as little as 97ms, and it streams in two ways. The right one depends on whether you have the full text when you make the request. When you have the full text: streaming over HTTP With stream=True and response_format="raw" , the speech API returns server-sent events as the audio is generated. Each conversation.item.audio_output.delta event carries a base64-encoded chunk of audio. This script receives the stream, decodes each chunk and saves the audio to a file. import base64
import json from together import Together client = Together() with client.audio.speech.with_streaming_response.create( model="Qwen/Qwen3-TTS-12Hz-1.7B", input="Your order has shipped and should arrive on Thursday.", voice="aiden", language="en", extra_body={"instructions": "excited and upbeat"}, stream=True, response_format="raw", response_encoding="pcm_s16le",
) as response, open("order-update.pcm", "wb") as audio: for line in response.iter_lines(): if not line.startswith("data: {"): continue event = json.loads(line[len("data: "):]) if event.get("type") == "conversation.item.audio_output.delta": # Each event carries a base64 chunk of 16-bit PCM at 24 kHz audio.write(base64.b64decode(event["delta"])) The output is 16-bit mono PCM at 24 kHz, and the instruction applies to a streamed request the same way it does to a complete one. To play speech as it arrives, send each decoded chunk to your audio output instead of a file. When the text is still being written: WebSocket If the text comes from a language model token by token, the WebSocket endpoint lets you send it in pieces. You open one connection, append text as it becomes available and commit when the input is complete, and the server synthesizes each sentence once it has the whole sentence. This script sends an order update in two pieces and saves the audio that comes back. It needs the websockets package, version 14 or later ( pip install websockets ). import asyncio
import base64
import json
import os import websockets URL = ( "wss://api.together.ai/v1/audio/speech/websocket" "?model=Qwen/Qwen3-TTS-12Hz-1.7B&voice=aiden&language=en" "&response_format=pcm&sample_rate=24000"
) async def main(): headers = {"Authorization": f"Bearer {os.environ['TOGETHER_API_KEY']}"} async with websockets.connect(URL, additional_headers=headers) as ws: # Append text as it becomes available, then commit once the input is complete await ws.send(json.

Summary Tencent Hy4 on Together AI: Tencent's flagship model is now available on Together Serverless Inference with a 1M-token context window. Image input: Hy4 is the first model in the Hy family to accept images alongside text, and it supports tool calling and JSON output. What we tested: Hy4 read the values off one of our charts, rebuilt the chart as a webpage, then wrote a working browser game from one prompt. Serverless pricing: Hy4 costs \$0.83 per 1M input tokens, \$0.042 per 1M cached input tokens and \$2.50 per 1M output tokens. Starting today, developers can run Tencent Hy4 on Together Serverless Inference. Hy4 is Tencent's flagship model and the first in the Hy family to accept images alongside text. We gave it two small jobs to see what it does in practice: extracting the data from one of our charts and rebuilding that chart in HTML, then writing a browser game from a single prompt. Here's what came back, along with the code to make the chart request yourself. It read our chart, then rebuilt it We started with vision, since that's the capability Hy4 adds. We took this chart from our Together Storage Accelerator post, gave Hy4 a screenshot of it, and asked for its contents as JSON. The response returned the title and all three values exactly as they appear in the chart: { "title": "A warm TSA read is 3.2 GB/s: 20x the R2 origin", "bars": [ { "label": "TSA warm read", "value": 3.18 }, { "label": "R2 origin, direct", "value": 0.16 }, { "label": "TSA cold read", "value": 0.14 } ]
} Next, we asked it to reproduce the chart as a single HTML file, using the same screenshot. This time, the output needed to preserve the visual layout as well as the numbers. The original is on top, and Hy4's version is below it. Hy4 reproduced the layout, the labels, the values, the colors, the dashed ceiling line and the footnote. We asked it to use generic fonts, so the typeface differs. It wrote a game For the second test, we gave Hy4 a text prompt rather than an image and asked it to write a browser game in one HTML file, with no libraries or external assets: Make a small, original browser game in a single HTML file, with no libraries and no external assets. Keyboard controls, a score, a way to lose and a restart. It should be fun for two minutes. Reply with only the HTML. Hy4 returned a game called Polarity. We saved the response as an HTML file and opened it in a browser without changing the code. Blocks fall from the top of the screen. You catch the ones that match your color and press Space to switch colors. A yellow hazard ends the run if it reaches you. Hy4 also added a title screen that explains the rules and a best score that carries over from one round to the next, neither of which we asked for. We played through a round, reached the game-over screen and restarted successfully. About Tencent Hy4 Hy4 is a Mixture-of-Experts model with 770B total parameters, 49B of them active per token. It follows Hy4 Preview, adding image input and computer-use capabilities. Tencent says it trained the model to operate professional software such as Blender and CAD tools. It also worked with its own product teams to train and evaluate Hy4 on office work, financial research and game development in Unreal Engine, Unity and Godot. Tencent's announcement and model card have the details. Run it on Together AI Hy4 is available now under the model string tencent/Hy4 , through Together's chat completions API. The example below is the chart request from earlier in this post. To run it, install the Together Python SDK ( pip install together ), set a TOGETHER_API_KEY , and save the chart we used as chart.png . import base64 from together import Together client = Together() with open("chart.png", "rb") as f: image = base64.b64encode(f.read()).decode() response = client.chat.completions.create( model="tencent/Hy4", messages=[ { "role": "user", "content": [ { "type": "text", "text": "Read this chart. Return a JSON object with a title " "and a list of bars, each with a label and a value.", }, { "type": "image_url", "image_url": {"url": f"data:image/png;base64,{image}"}, }, ], } ], response_format={"type": "json_object"},
) print(response.choices[0].message.content) If you'd like to try Hy4 before writing any code, you can open it in the playground , and the model page has the full details. Available now Run Tencent Hy4 on Together AI 1M-token context, text and image input, tool calling and JSON output, on serverless inference. Open the playground FAQs How much does Tencent Hy4 cost on Together AI? Tencent Hy4 on Together AI costs \$0.83 per 1M input tokens, \$2.50 per 1M output tokens and \$0.042 per 1M cached input tokens. What is the context window of Tencent Hy4 on Together AI? Tencent Hy4 has a 1M-token context window on Together AI, and you call it with the model string tencent/Hy4 . Does Tencent Hy4 accept images? Yes, Tencent Hy4 accepts text and image input on Together AI and returns text, and it's the first model in Tencent's Hy family with vision. You send each image as an image_url block in the message content, as in the example above. Does Tencent Hy4 support JSON output and tool calling? Yes, Tencent Hy4 supports both on Together AI. You get JSON output by setting response_format to {"type": "json_object"} , and you enable tool calling by passing your functions in the standard tools parameter.
